# C# Documentation Standards

Defines mandatory XML documentation standards for all C# scripts in Spellwright, following official C# guidelines and Unity/DOTS best practices.

## Core Principles

### XML Documentation Format

All public and internal types, members, and methods MUST use C# XML documentation with triple-slash `///` format.

### Mandatory Documentation Targets

**MUST be documented:**

- All public classes, structs, interfaces
- All public and internal methods
- All public properties and fields
- All ECS components (IComponentData, IBufferElementData)
- All ECS systems (ISystem, SystemBase)
- All Jobs (IJob, IJobEntity)
- All enum types and values
- All delegates and events

**Optional documentation:**

- Private methods (recommended for complex logic)
- Private fields (only if non-obvious)

### Required Tags

**Minimum tags per member:**

- `<summary>` - Brief description (1-3 sentences)
- `<param>` - For each method parameter
- `<returns>` - For non-void methods
- `<exception>` - For methods that throw
- `<remarks>` - For implementation details (when needed)

## Standard XML Tags

| Tag                  | Purpose                | Required For       | Usage                                |
| -------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------ | ------------------------------------ |
| `<summary>`          | Brief description      | All public APIs    | 1-3 sentences describing purpose     |
| `<param>`            | Parameter description  | All parameters     | Describe purpose and valid values    |
| `<returns>`          | Return value           | Non-void methods   | Describe what is returned            |
| `<exception>`        | Exception docs         | Methods that throw | Document exception conditions        |
| `<remarks>`          | Implementation details | Complex logic      | System group, threading, performance |
| `<value>`            | Property value         | Properties         | Describe value and valid range       |
| `<c>`                | Inline code term       | In text            | Reference code elements inline       |
| `<see cref=""/>`     | Code reference         | Cross-references   | Link to related types/members        |
| `<seealso cref=""/>` | Related reference      | Related links      | Link to related documentation        |

## Writing Guidelines

### Summary Tag

**DO:**

- Start with active verbs: "Represents", "Defines", "Calculates", "Processes"
- Use present tense
- Keep to 1-3 sentences
- Include scope and purpose
- Mention valid ranges for numeric values

**DON'T:**

- Repeat the member name
- Write vague descriptions
- Use passive voice
- Exceed 3 sentences

### Param Tag

**DO:**

- Start with "The" or "A/An"
- Describe purpose and valid values
- Match parameter name exactly
- Document constraints and ranges

**DON'T:**

- Just repeat the parameter type
- Skip validation requirements
- Use ambiguous descriptions

### Returns Tag

**DO:**

- Describe what is returned, not just type
- Include range or possible values
- Mention null conditions
- Document special return values

**DON'T:**

- Just state the type
- Skip null documentation
- Omit range information

### Remarks Tag

**DO:**

- Document system group and update order
- Specify Burst compilation status
- Describe threading model (Parallel/Sequential)
- Include performance characteristics (O(n), target ms)
- Document which systems read/write components
- Explain architectural decisions

**DON'T:**

- Duplicate summary information
- Skip performance documentation
- Omit system dependencies

## Unity-Specific Patterns

### MonoBehaviour/ScriptableObject

**Required documentation:**

- Component purpose and attachment location
- Required dependencies
- Inspector-configurable fields with ranges
- Unity messages used (Awake, Start, Update)

### ECS Components

**Required documentation:**

- What the component represents
- Which systems read/write it
- Performance characteristics (blittable, burst-compatible)
- Data constraints and valid ranges
- Architecture layer (Generator/Modifier/Payload)

### ECS Systems

**Required documentation:**

- System purpose (verb-based)
- Components queried
- System group: `[UpdateInGroup(typeof(...))]`
- Update order: `[UpdateBefore/After(typeof(...))]`
- Burst status: Yes/No
- Threading: Parallel (IJobEntity) or Sequential
- Performance: O(n) complexity, target ms

### ECS Jobs

**Required documentation:**

- Job purpose and execution model
- Burst compilation: Yes/No
- Component access: RW/RO for each component
- Thread safety: Parallel safe or sequential only
- Performance target

## Spellwright Architecture

### Generator/Modifier/Payload Pattern

**Required in remarks:**

- Architecture layer (Generator/Modifier/Payload)
- Compatibility with other layers
- Performance characteristics
- Valid value ranges
- Recommended values

### JSON Baking Systems

**Required in remarks:**

- System type: Baking System
- Execution timing: One-time during creation
- JSON schema path
- Output components
- Validation layer

## Advanced Patterns

### Performance Documentation

**Include in remarks:**

- Time complexity: O(n), O(n\*m), O(1)
- Memory allocation: Zero GC, NativeArrays
- Burst compilation: Yes/No
- Threading model: Parallel/Sequential
- Target metrics: <0.5ms for 1000 entities @60fps
- Profiler marker name

### Thread Safety

**Include in remarks:**

- Component access patterns: RO/RW
- Parallel safety: Yes/No
- Race condition prevention: ECB, no shared writes
- Synchronization: Implicit via job scheduling

### Validation and Constraints

**Include in remarks:**

- Valid ranges: 1.0f to 1000.0f
- Recommended values: 10f to 100f
- Constraints: Must be positive, non-zero, finite
- Validation layer: Client-side, LLM, Schema

## Documentation Checklist

Before submitting code, verify:

- [ ] All public types have `<summary>`
- [ ] All parameters have `<param>`
- [ ] All non-void methods have `<returns>`
- [ ] All exceptions have `<exception>`
- [ ] Cross-references use correct `<see cref=""/>` syntax
- [ ] ECS components document which systems use them
- [ ] ECS systems document group and update order
- [ ] Performance-critical code has performance remarks
- [ ] Unity attributes are explained in remarks
- [ ] No spelling or grammar errors
- [ ] Documentation matches current implementation

## Tools and Validation

**IDE Integration:**

- Visual Studio: Enable CS1591 warnings
- Rider: Enable "Missing XML comment" inspections
- VS Code: Install C# XML Documentation extension

**Build-Time:**

- Treat documentation warnings as errors in CI/CD

**AI-Assisted:**

- Review for technical accuracy
- Verify code references
- Validate performance claims
- Check Unity/DOTS terminology

## Best Practices

**DO:**

- Write for developers unfamiliar with code
- Include scope and valid ranges
- Document performance characteristics
- Explain architectural decisions
- Keep synchronized with code changes
- Use proper XML syntax
- Document "why", not just "what"

**DON'T:**

- Write redundant descriptions
- Use vague language
- Forget XML escaping for generics: `<see cref="List{T}"/>`
- Copy-paste without reviewing
- Leave documentation outdated
- Skip performance documentation for DOTS code

